# Panchayat Bill
Tamil title: ஊராட்சி மன்றச் சட்ட முன்வடிவு

**C. Subramaniam:** I would say that so long as people fear that they will be carried away in that fashion, it may be better for us not even to consider a Panchayat Bill. Only when our people acquire the resolve to say, "We have no fear that anyone will carry us away; we possess the courage to cast our votes freely," can this democratic system be soundly established. No form of government and no democratic institution can succeed while people fear that they will be carried away or remain in a condition where they vote out of fear of another person's influence.

I, however, have great faith in the people. Although a few may behave wrongly, I have no doubt that 999 out of every thousand possess the courage and ability to act freely according to their own wishes.

**C. N. Annadurai:** I am glad that the Finance Minister has taken this opportunity to express his faith in the people.

**C. Subramaniam:** I thank you for giving me the opportunity.

**C. N. Annadurai:** If it is said that people cannot now be carried away, I believe they have not forgotten that Sita, unable to protect herself, was carried away by Ravana.

**C. Subramaniam:** That was in those days. It cannot happen today.

**Mr. Speaker:** The Honourable Annadurai is giving even me an opportunity to interrupt his speech. [Laughter.] If he speaks only about the bill, I wish to point out that such interruptions will not arise.

**C. N. Annadurai:** The Finance Minister says that what happened in those days will not happen today. Today, instead of carrying away Sita, they carry away Sitaramans. [Laughter.]

**S. Nagaraja Manickar:** On a point of order. In Tamil Nadu, those who sang *Keemayanam*, demanding that the Ramayana itself be burned, now speak with belief in Sita and Rama. I ask whether they possess such belief.

**Mr. Speaker:** I don't see any point of order in this. I rule it out of order. The Hon. Member may continue his speech.

**C. N. Annadurai:** I wish to explain why we raise in this House the disorders that arise under a system in which the panchayat members elect the panchayat president. This very House previously resolved that the general public should elect the president. Later, considering that system unsuccessful in practice, this House itself changed it and introduced the method under which the members elect the president.

If it now becomes necessary to change this arrangement and restore the earlier method, we fear that the rulers may look at us and say, "You have not shown us the reasons that make such a change necessary." That is why we are placing these disorders before the House now.

The faults in the way democracy is practised are the fundamental reasons defects arise when bills are prepared. Under today's democratic procedure, the Government has no opportunity to consult the opposition parties or others while drafting a bill. Therefore, in the present circumstances, bills must be supplied to the parties. When the village people look at us and ask, "What does this new bill give us?" it gives us no opportunity to answer, "From today you will receive powers you did not possess until yesterday," or, "Your status has now been raised."

---NOTES---
`Sitaramans` preserves Anna's joke: instead of the epic Sita being carried away, contemporary people bearing the name Sitaraman are carried off during electoral manoeuvring. `கீமாயணம்` is transliterated as *Keemayanam* because it is a printed title/reference in the source and is not silently normalized.
